_adjustment": -0.57, "key_factors": ["Unanimous Safe/Solid D expert ratings across nine raters", "Incumbent Scholten holds a large cash advantage ($1.36M vs $361K) with no outside opposition spending", "District's modest Democratic lean plus a favorable national environment", "DeBoer, a primary outsider, faces an acknowledged uphill path"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a comfortable incumbent win: a D+4 district, a favorable environment, Scholten's fundraising edge, and no outside money against her. Every major rater calls the race Safe or Solid D, and markets price her near 93-94%, so the forecast stays at a double-digit Democratic margin.", "watch": "A strong late Republican push in Kent County, or any polling showing DeBoer closing within single digits, would warrant moving this toward Likely D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Hillary Scholten (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Hillary Scholten seeks re-election in a West Michigan district that leans Democratic by about four points, facing Republican nominee Terri DeBoer, a former meteorologist who won the primary as a political outsider. Scholten has out-raised DeBoer roughly four to one and holds more than $1.3 million on hand, with no outside spending targeting her so far. DeBoer has tried to distance herself from her party on some tax and spending issues, but coverage describes her path as difficult.\n\nWith no public general-election polling, the race assessment rests on fundamentals and expert judgment. All nine ratings tracked call the race Safe or Solid for Democrats, and prediction markets price Scholten's win probability in the low-to-mid nineties. The expected margin is a comfortable double-digit Democratic win, with residual uncertainty reflecting the absence of direct polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9846, "margin": 15.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23",